{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,90p;555,640p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '1,220p';\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,90p;555,640p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '1,220p';\"","aggregated_output":"     632 docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md\n     181 docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md\n     194 docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md\n      56 docs/design/dao.shibi.md\n     268 docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md\n    1331 total\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, which is deprecated whole once this is\n     8\timplemented (section 8). Implementation order, slices and the\n     9\tcompanion-edit inventory live in\n    10\t[`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n    11\t\n    12\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    13\t\n    14\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    15\t\n    16\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    17\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    18\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    19\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    20\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    21\t\n    22\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    23\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    24\t> client.\n    25\t\n    26\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    27\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    28\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    29\tcontract beyond the amendments section 9 lists (the three accepted decisions\n    30\tOD-1, OD-2 and OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    31\tsentence).\n    32\t\n    33\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    34\t\n    35\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    36\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n    37\t  reachable address is required (section 4).\n    38\t- The unreachable cases are named by mapping and filtering behavior, not\n    39\t  product name: endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a\n    40\t  symmetric NAT; the strict paths of a carrier-grade NAT, which is address\n    41\t  sharing and not one behavior, since other carrier-grade paths hole-punch)\n    42\t  defeats punching, and relay is the workaround (section 4).\n    43\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    44\t- Every peer needs an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay.\n    45\t  If UDP is blocked and no such path exists, communication is impossible.\n    46\t- Everything a channel carries is readable by every peer that carries or\n    47\t  receives it until encryption is composed, and the encryption this version\n    48\t  specifies is value-level only: a relay still sees every envelope,\n    49\t  identities, ops, cursors, ids, sizes and timing included (section 7).\n    50\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    51\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    52\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    53\t\n    54\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    55\tnames the seam; `dao.shibi.md` owns the rest); channel-level encryption\n    56\t(deferred, section 7); WebRTC data channels; browser inbound; readiness\n    57\tnotification; exactly-once delivery.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n    60\t\n    61\tOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\n    62\tas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\n    63\toperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\n    64\toutcome maps returned verbatim. Four concepts.\n    65\t\n    66\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    67\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    68\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    69\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    70\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    71\t  peer (3.3).\n    72\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    73\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    74\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    75\t  state).\n    76\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    77\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    78\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    79\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    80\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    81\t  share.\n    82\t\n    83\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    84\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    85\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    86\tend.\n    87\t\n    88\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    89\t\n    90\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n   555\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   556\t\n   557\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\n   558\tthe channel's `end` (2.4); the table entry persists, so a holder that\n   559\treconnects within duration plus tolerance finds its entry, its cursors and\n   560\tits lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a\n   561\thealthy channel. Only expiry frees anything. A meeting peer that restarts\n   562\tloses its table and ledger, so every pair is gone, holders observe\n   563\t`not-found` and re-propose; this is `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant\n   564\twith an empty inventory, correct because the table is process-scoped.\n   565\t\n   566\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   567\t\n   568\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   569\t\n   570\tThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\n   571\tpure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\n   572\thandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\n   573\tmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\n   574\ttouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\n   575\tdocument adds the network attachment points: the **table entry** (the\n   576\tserved handle, before it enters the table; the mirror sees only the wrapped\n   577\thandle; gating, value encryption for serving, redaction, metering), the\n   578\t**reflection** (inside the host-composed attach closure; presenting a\n   579\tcredential, value decryption, metering), and the **channel** (reserved for\n   580\ta channel-level wrapper, deferred below).\n   581\t\n   582\tKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\n   583\tfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\n   584\tcursors, sizes and timing visible. **Channel-level encryption is deferred:\n   585\tthis version specifies no wrapper at the channel attachment point**, its\n   586\tinterface and its ordering against codec and fragmentation (encode, then\n   587\tencrypt, then fragment) being requirements on that future design, not a\n   588\tdesign here. The exact consequence: a relay peer that carries requests and\n   589\tanswers sees every envelope in the clear, identities, ops, cursors, ids,\n   590\tsizes and timing included, and any part of a value not hidden by composed\n   591\tvalue-level middleware. Key distribution is a convention and is not\n   592\tspecified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   593\t\n   594\t**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi\n   595\t(`dao.shibi.md`), unspecified. This document offers only the slot, a\n   596\treserved open key on the request map `:dao.stream.remote/credential` whose\n   597\tvalue is opaque here and travels per request (a per-channel credential is a\n   598\tconvention: the reflection's `present` middleware attaches the same value\n   599\tto every request); the context, `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment\n   600\tidentity>}` handed by the mirror to `apply-request` while local callers\n   601\thand `{}` and no other context key exists in this version; the gate,\n   602\tmirror-side `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify` is a pure function\n   603\tof the latest decision, context and request (the decisions are published to\n   604\ta capacity-1 medium by an index interpreter the composition runs over its\n   605\tfact streams) and whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused`\n   606\twith an opaque `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`; and the presenter,\n   607\treflection-side `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a pure function of context\n   608\tand request that fills the slot.\n   609\t\n   610\tA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\n   611\twithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the interpreter\n   612\tthat folds the streams it observes and publishes decisions onto the\n   613\tdecision medium, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`, its tokens\n   614\tare the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are the gate's\n   615\tdecision emissions onto a stream the index also observes. Limitation,\n   616\tstated plainly: the seam carries the index's latest decision per medium,\n   617\tnot the history it folded; a query that must reason over history folds it\n   618\tin the index and publishes a derived decision. Until ShiBi exists, gating\n   619\tis an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared\n   620\tvalue in the slot.\n   621\t\n   622\tLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\n   623\tcredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n   624\t\n   625\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   626\t\n   627\t`dao.stream.remote` replaces `dao.stream.serve.md` and succeeds\n   628\t`dao.jing.remote` whole; the successor path and the fate of each existing\n   629\tnetwork mechanism are recorded in\n   630\t[`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n   631\tThe only `dao.stream.md` changes are the five amendments listed there;\n   632\tthey add no network or capability concept to the contract.\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote Implementation Plan\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: plan, not rules. The rules are\n     4\t[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md); this file carries the\n     5\tmigration design, the completion criteria and slices, and the companion-edit\n     6\tinventory moved out of that spec to keep it short. Subordinate to the spec\n     7\ton any disagreement.\n     8\t\n     9\t## 1. What replaces `dao.jing.remote`: `dao.jing.content`\n    10\t\n    11\tContent lookup is a request and response service over remote streams\n    12\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 5), in one module family, `dao.jing.content`:\n    13\tthe namespaces `dao.jing.content` (`serve-step`), `dao.jing.content.step`\n    14\t(`step`), `dao.jing.content.driver` (`driver`, clj only) and\n    15\t`dao.jing.content.async` (`async`).\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n    18\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n    19\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n    20\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n    21\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text in\n    22\t  the application value; message boundaries are the channel codec's, one\n    23\t  application value per message, so the vocabulary rides Transit-JSON text\n    24\t  or canonical CBOR alike (`dao.jing.cbor.md`, Remote and DHT).\n    25\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n    26\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n    27\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n    28\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n    29\t  moved from today's private `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by\n    30\t  the interpreter, by the stepped client on every `get` answer and\n    31\t  `:present` verify, and by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one\n    32\t  (today: `dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `dao/jing/remote/step.cljc`, and the\n    33\t  linker's own; only the first includes the canonical CBOR decode).\n    34\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n    35\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n    36\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n    37\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n    38\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n    39\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n    40\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n    41\t  `dao.jing` handle. It replaces `connect-content!`; its consumers are the\n    42\t  coordinate opener and, through it, `dao.space` (the published-index\n    43\t  coordinate opens it at `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`) and `yin.repl.link`\n    44\t  on the JVM. `yin.vm.content` has no remote path today (its handle is an\n    45\t  argument), so it gains one only by being handed a coordinate-built handle.\n    46\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n    47\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API;\n    48\t  `dao.data.btree.storage` is the consumer (`hydrate-async`,\n    49\t  `store-tree-async`).\n    50\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n    51\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n    52\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n    53\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone; its consumers are\n    54\t  the tests and the coordinate itself.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 2. Unification and contract boundary\n    57\t\n    58\tFates of the current network paths when remote streams land:\n    59\t\n    60\t- `dao.stream.ws` and its jvm, node, browser and dart adapters: subsumed;\n    61\t  the attachment, writer, deposit medium and projection form a channel.\n    62\t- `dao.stream.serving`: retired; it forwards copies and flattens source gaps.\n    63\t  Its consumers, `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote`, move in slices 4-5.\n    64\t- `dao.stream.forward`: unrelated; it remains a local copy step.\n    65\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: convention over streams; its correlation vocabulary may\n    66\t  remain above reflections.\n    67\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: retired; the WebSocket projection binds the channel.\n    68\t- `dao.stream.apply` wire envelope: retired; the AST node and VM FFI bridge\n    69\t  are unrelated and keep their names.\n    70\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter` and `daostream:ws://`:\n    71\t  convention over request and answer streams; the URL becomes a pair\n    72\t  descriptor. Shared-shell access remains interpreter policy.\n    73\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async` and its coordinate:\n    74\t  deprecated whole; `dao.jing.content` succeeds them in slice 4.\n    75\t- `yin.repl.link` and linker M3/M4: convention over reflections or local\n    76\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core remains.\n    77\t- B-tree `hydrate-async` and `store-tree-async`: convention over the content\n    78\t  client; its callback facade remains host policy under OD-5.\n    79\t- The UDP DHT remains unrelated with its Kademlia envelope; its datagram\n    80\t  budget informs the new UDP channel.\n    81\t- Transit and CBOR remain codecs used by channels.\n    82\t\n    83\tThe `dao.stream.md` changes are the only changes to that contract: OD-1,\n    84\tOD-2 and OD-3; the `:dao.stream/refused` rows on `cursor`, `next` and\n    85\t`append!`; and the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces. They add no network\n    86\tor capability concept. Companion-document edits are listed in section 4.\n    87\t\n    88\t## 3. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n    89\t\n    90\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n    91\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n    92\t\n    93\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    94\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n    95\t+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n    96\t| 0  | Contract and source alignment: the `dao.stream.md`         | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and the   | clj cljs cljd |\n    97\t|    | amendments of the spec's section 9 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\n    98\t|    | `:dao.stream/refused` added to the closed outcome sets     | throw; the outcome sets admit the refusal.                   |               |\n    99\t|    | for `cursor`, `next` and `append!` in                      |                                                              |               |\n   100\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`; `dao.stream.observe`,          |                                                              |               |\n   101\t|    | `yin.vm.engine` follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.      |                                                              |               |\n   102\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   103\t| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; a     | clj cljs cljd |\n   104\t|    | `present`, a metering exemplar, an index-interpreter        | gate with the channel allow-list refuses with                |               |\n   105\t|    | exemplar publishing decisions. `src/cljc/dao/stream/        | `:dao.stream/refused`; a gate reads the latest decision      |               |\n   106\t|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | through a capacity-1 medium after eviction; a filter cannot  |               |\n   107\t|    |                                                            | be expressed.                                                |               |\n   108\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   109\t| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the channel;     | clj cljs cljd |\n   110\t|    | remote descriptor dispatch, the protocol errors.           | `gap` from an evicting source crosses verbatim with the      |               |\n   111\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, tests.                  | source's cursor; `blocked` then `ok` on re-ask; `not-found`  |               |\n   112\t|    |                                                            | marks gone; `no-surface` does not; the descriptor answer     |               |\n   113\t|    |                                                            | carries the surface; a kept cursor from a reflection is      |               |\n   114\t|    |                                                            | accepted by the source handle.                               |               |\n   115\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   116\t| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: `ws-project` at each end;   | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj, cljd    | clj cljs cljd |\n   117\t|    | mirror steps over accepted and dialed attachments;         | to clj; both directions serve on one channel; browser        |               |\n   118\t|    | retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and the served-path table    | dials and reads; a closed connection is observed as the      |               |\n   119\t|    | in `dao.stream.ws`.                                        | link's `end`.                                                |               |\n   120\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   121\t| 4  | Content service: the `dao.jing.content` family (section 1) | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the new      | clj cljs cljd |\n   122\t|    | with `serve-step`, `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`;         | module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the linker M3    |               |\n   123\t|    | `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; `dao.jing.coordinate` new         | and M4 tests unchanged in outcome; no require of             |               |\n   124\t|    | coordinate; delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`,        | `dao.jing.remote` remains.                                   |               |\n   125\t|    | `remote/step.cljc`, `remote/async.cljc` and their tests.   |                                                              |               |\n   126\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   127\t| 5  | REPL as a service: `yin.repl.serve` and `connect` over     | `(connect \"daostream:ws://...\")` resolves to a remote        | clj cljs cljd |\n   128\t|    | `requests` and `answers` reflections; delete               | descriptor pair; the demo REPLs work; `yin.repl.link` serves |               |\n   129\t|    | `dao.stream.serving`, `dao.stream.rpc.ws`, the apply wire  | content through slice 4; `dao.stream.rpc` is reworked over   |               |\n   130\t|    | envelope from `dao.stream.apply`; rework or retire         | reflections or retired in the same slice, because the apply  |               |\n   131\t|    | `dao.stream.rpc`.                                          | envelope it requires is deleted here.                        |               |\n   132\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   133\t| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation keyed by  | The toy over UDP with a 100 KiB value; loss injection       | clj cljs cljd |\n   134\t|    | attachment, address, direction and id; adapters            | recovers by resend; oversize beyond the maximum answers      |               |\n   135\t|    | `src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/           | `transport-error` with reason `oversize`.                    |               |\n   136\t|    | stream/udp/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd`.|                                                              |               |\n   137\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   138\t| 7  | Pair channel and the meeting and relay conventions with    | Two peers behind a simulated restricted NAT punch; behind    | clj           |\n   139\t|    | leases: `dao.stream.remote.pair`, `dao.stream.remote.meet`;| a simulated symmetric NAT they relay; a pair whose holder    |               |\n   140\t|    | a meeting gate refuses past the pair bound.                | stops renewing answers `not-found` after duration plus       |               |\n   141\t|    |                                                            | tolerance; a reconnect within it finds the pair intact; a    |               |\n   142\t|    |                                                            | meeting peer past its bound refuses, not silences.          |               |\n   143\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   144\t| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates with a   | clj cljs cljd |\n   145\t|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields `gap` with   |               |\n   146\t|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | the source's cursor.                                         |               |\n   147\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   148\t\n   149\tDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n   150\t(ShiBi), key distribution, channel-level encryption, a WebRTC data channel,\n   151\tbrowser inbound. None changes a shape in the spec's section 2.\n   152\t\n   153\t## 4. Companion-edit inventory\n   154\t\n   155\tThe edits the unification requires in other design documents, beyond the\n   156\tspec's own sections. Those not yet made are slices; those made in the\n   157\tauthoring and fix rounds are listed as done.\n   158\t\n   159\t- `dao.stream.md` (done): OD-1, OD-2, OD-3 accepted and moved into the\n   160\t  contract body; the `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and\n   161\t  `append!`; the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces; the OD-3 wording\n   162\t  corrected so `attach!`'s `not-found` and a stale cursor are separate\n   163\t  cases.\n   164\t- `dao.lease.md` (done): served entries and relay pairs named as lease\n   165\t  subjects; the carriage note names the `not-found` protocol error.\n   166\t- `dao.stream.ws.md` (done): the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   167\t  the copy path versus attachment to the original; dialer-side and\n   168\t  acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption and RPC items point to the\n   169\t  spec.\n   170\t- `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (done): 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite\n   171\t  the spec; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's source outcome;\n   172\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the acceptance\n   173\t  row cites slice 8 here.\n   174\t- `dao.jing.md` (done): the coordinate shape and its today/target split;\n   175\t  the deprecated backend bullet; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` marked as the\n   176\t  target; the Base64 carriage named as channel-codec message boundaries.\n   177\t- `yin.vm.linker.md` (done): the content pair vocabulary cites section 1\n   178\t  here; the 6.1 table row, 6.3 shape, M3, deps and failure-policy timing.\n   179\t- `dao.data.btree.md` (done): the 5.4 backend row annotated as the target\n   180\t  name for `dao.jing.remote`; the Phase 4 landing note.\n   181\t- `dao.jing.dht.md` (done): the NAT limitation.\n   182\t- `daostream-udp-design.md` (done): superseded status line.\n   183\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (done): the framing claims corrected to channel-codec\n   184\t  message boundaries with Base64 in the application value.\n   185\t- `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, `dao.space.query.md`,\n   186\t  `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md`\n   187\t  (done): one status sentence each naming the successor.\n   188\t- `dao.jing.hash-registry.md`, `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`\n   189\t  (done): one-line pointers that the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe\n   190\t  today's code and the successor keeps the checks and classifications.\n   191\t- Remaining, with the code: the `dao.stream.ws.md` residual dialer/acceptor\n   192\t  vocabulary beside its older deposit-model wording, and `dao.stream.md`'s\n   193\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   194\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
