{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '95,150p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '18,60p'; nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '237,251p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,36p;395,414p;485,504p;568,620p'; git diff --check; git diff --unified=0 -- docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | awk '/\"'^'\"\\\\+[\"'^+]/ && length($0)>81 {print length($0)-1 \":\" substr($0,2)}'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '95,150p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '18,60p'; nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '237,251p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,36p;395,414p;485,504p;568,620p'; git diff --check; git diff --unified=0 -- docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | awk '/\"'^'\"\\\\+[\"'^+]/ && length($0)>81 {print length($0)-1 \":\" substr($0,2)}'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"     600 docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md\n    95\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    96\t\n    97\t## Two generic middlewares\n    98\t\n    99\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n   100\tspecified here.\n   101\t\n   102\t```clojure\n   103\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify   verify\n   104\t       :dao.stream.middleware/decision h})\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   108\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   109\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   110\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling.\n   111\t\n   112\t`wrap` calls `cursor` with `:dao.stream/oldest` once when it constructs\n   113\tthe gate. The decision medium must be open and readable;\n   114\tif cursor minting is not `ok`, that gate starts unavailable and retries\n   115\tcursor minting once on each operation. A successful mint is its read\n   116\tcursor. The medium has capacity one and evicts its older value.\n   117\t\n   118\tOn each operation, the gate calls `next` once at its read cursor. On `ok`,\n   119\tit stores the successor cursor and value. On `blocked`, it keeps the cursor\n   120\tand last value. On `end`, it clears the value and becomes ended. On `gap`,\n   121\tit clears the value, adopts the recovery cursor, and calls `next` once more.\n   122\tIf that call is `ok`, it stores the successor and value; if `blocked`, it\n   123\tkeeps the recovery cursor without a value; if `end`, it becomes ended; if\n   124\t`gap`, it adopts that recovery cursor and remains without a value. It does\n   125\tnot read a third time in this operation. On any other non-`ok` outcome,\n   126\tit clears the value and cursor, then retries cursor minting next operation.\n   127\t\n   128\t`verify` receives the last value observed, or\n   129\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}` when there is none. That value is a\n   130\tbounded snapshot, not a promise that it is the newest concurrent write.\n   131\t`nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits with\n   132\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   133\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   134\trefuses the marker. The cursor and value are state of the\n   135\twrapped handle, supplied to `wrap`, not global state. The composition must\n   136\tserialize calls on one wrapped handle; the gate has no queue, lock or wait.\n   137\tThe decision medium is not the wrapped stream, so the position rule on the\n   138\tinner handle is untouched.\n   139\t\n   140\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   141\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   142\tit was composed with: this is **decision emission**, and an index that\n   143\tobserves that stream is how metered use keeps its state on a stream\n   144\trather than in the gate.\n   145\t\n   146\t**present**, the credential step.\n   147\t\n   148\t```clojure\n   149\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   150\t```\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify` adapter, a pure function of the latest decision, context and\n    26\t  request that answers nil or a reason. This is the query interpreter's\n    27\t  middleware contract, not the query interpreter itself;\n    28\t- an index interpreter over its fact streams, folding with its own cursors\n    29\t  and publishing decisions onto a decision medium a gate reads\n    30\t  (`dao.stream.middleware.md`, gate);\n    31\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    32\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    33\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    34\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    35\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    36\t\n    37\tThe seam directly supports a query interpreter only when it can implement\n    38\t`verify` from one bounded decision snapshot and the request. A general query\n    39\tinterpreter that must query tuple streams needs an additional composition\n    40\tthat runs it over those streams and makes its request-specific result\n    41\tavailable to `verify`. That query execution and its result medium are not\n    42\tspecified here. ShiBi must define them before relying on general tuple-space\n    43\tqueries for authorization; the index and query interpreters remain separate.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    46\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    47\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    48\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    49\t\n    50\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    51\t\n    52\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    53\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    54\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    55\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n    56\tgating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n   237\t## The minimum viable stack\n   238\t\n   239\tFor a working multi-agent system on today's code, trusted agents only — no coordinator, no\n   240\tdeposit API, no new namespaces:\n   241\t\n   242\t1. **Historical implementation:** today's `dao.jing.file` content handle\n   243\t   used `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers` and\n   244\t   `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`. `dao.jing.remote` is deprecated\n   245\t   whole; the planned successor is `dao.jing.content` over\n   246\t   `dao.stream.remote.md` (see its implementation plan). Both handles\n   247\t   expose the same plain-data content effects.\n   248\t2. **Writes**: each agent owns a local `dao.stream` memory-log and creates one\n   249\t   transactor value with that stream plus an explicit DaoJing intake pool\n   250\t   (`dao.space.transactor/create!`). It commits entity maps or datom vectors\n   251\t   through `transactor/append!`/`transact!`, using integer stream-local entity ids; the\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, deprecated whole when this lands (plan section 1).\n     8\tImplementation order, slices and the\n     9\tcompanion-edit inventory live in\n    10\t[`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n    11\t\n    12\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    13\t\n    14\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    15\t\n    16\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    17\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    18\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    19\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    20\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    21\t\n    22\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    23\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    24\t> client.\n    25\t\n    26\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    27\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    28\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    29\tcontract beyond the five amendments listed in implementation plan section 2\n    30\t(OD-1, OD-2, OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    31\tsentence).\n    32\t\n    33\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    34\t\n    35\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    36\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n   395\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   396\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   397\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   398\t```\n   399\t\n   400\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   401\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   402\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   403\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   404\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   405\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   406\tunder the inboxes' retention. The pair adapter turns `gap` on `in` into\n   407\t`end` for that link, terminating the affected channel. The link applies its\n   408\texisting resend rule to outstanding reads and reports unknown append\n   409\teffects on its event writer. The inner reader never sees the channel's\n   410\t`gap` as the source's `gap` (2.4). Double\n   411\tframing consumes budget: an inner value inside an outer `append!` must fit\n   412\tthe outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   413\t\n   414\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   485\tit was a string.\n   486\t\n   487\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   488\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   489\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   490\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   491\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   492\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   493\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   494\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   495\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   496\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   497\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1).\n   498\t\n   499\t**Meeting board and relay pair.** Section 4.\n   500\t\n   501\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   502\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   503\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   504\t\n   568\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   569\t\n   570\tMiddleware preserves positions and outcome kinds as specified in\n   571\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`. Network attachment points are the table entry\n   572\t(gate, value encryption, redaction or metering), the reflection (credential\n   573\tpresentation, value decryption or metering), and the channel (reserved for\n   574\tdeferred channel-level wrapping).\n   575\t\n   576\tKeys and chains are composition data, never descriptor fields. Value-level\n   577\tencryption hides values only; identity, ops, cursors, sizes and timing stay\n   578\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   579\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   580\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   581\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   582\t\n   583\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   584\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   585\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   586\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision.\n   587\t`dao.shibi.md` defines the limit: `verify` adapts a query interpreter to\n   588\tone bounded snapshot, not general tuple-space queries. General query\n   589\texecution and request-specific results are deferred; no wire shape changes.\n   590\t\n   591\tLease attribution uses per-author media and needs no credential. A gate on\n   592\ta renewal medium is an optional composition.\n   593\t\n   594\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   595\t\n   596\t`dao.stream.remote` replaces the old serve draft and succeeds\n   597\t`dao.jing.remote` whole. The `dao.jing.content` successor and vocabulary\n   598\t(implementation plan, section 1), mechanism fates and five network-free\n   599\tcontract amendments are listed in the\n   600\t[`implementation plan`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
