{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"section [89]|Section [89]|## [89]\\\\.\\\" docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,34p;400,415p;485,505p;565,610p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '107,145p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '18,55p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '1,95p;150,205p'; nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '237,251p'; wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; git diff --check; git diff --unified=0 -- docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | awk '/\"'^'\"\\\\+[\"'^+]/ { line=substr($0,2); if (length(line)>80) print \"WIDE \" length(line) \":\" line; if (line ~ /[''^ -~]/) print \"NONASCII:\" line }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"section [89]|Section [89]|## [89]\\\\.\\\" docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,34p;400,415p;485,505p;565,610p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '107,145p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '18,55p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '1,95p;150,205p'; nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '237,251p'; wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; git diff --check; git diff --unified=0 -- docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | awk '/\"'^'\"\\\\+[\"'^+]/ { line=substr($0,2); if (length(line)>80) print \"WIDE \" length(line) \":\" line; if (line ~ /[''^ -~]/) print \"NONASCII:\" line }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:592:## 8. Unification\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:596:## 9. Contract boundary\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:97:|    | amendments of the spec's section 9 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:204:Moved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, deprecated whole when this lands (plan section 1).\n     8\tSlices and companion edits are in\n     9\t[`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    12\t\n    13\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    14\t\n    15\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    16\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    17\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    18\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    19\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    20\t\n    21\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    22\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    23\t> client.\n    24\t\n    25\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    26\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    27\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    28\tcontract beyond the five amendments listed in implementation plan section 2\n    29\t(OD-1, OD-2, OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    30\tsentence).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    33\t\n    34\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   400\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   401\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   402\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   403\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   404\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   405\tunder the inboxes' retention. The pair adapter turns `gap` on `in` into\n   406\t`end` for that link, terminating the affected channel. The link applies its\n   407\texisting resend rule to outstanding reads and reports unknown append\n   408\teffects on its event writer. The inner reader never sees the channel's\n   409\t`gap` as the source's `gap` (2.4). Double\n   410\tframing consumes budget: an inner value inside an outer `append!` must fit\n   411\tthe outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   412\t\n   413\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   414\t\n   415\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   485\t\n   486\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   487\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   488\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   489\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   490\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   491\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   492\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   493\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   494\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   495\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   496\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1).\n   497\t\n   498\t**Meeting board and relay pair.** Section 4.\n   499\t\n   500\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   501\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   502\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   503\t\n   504\t## 6. Lease integration\n   505\t\n   565\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   566\t\n   567\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   568\t\n   569\tMiddleware preserves positions and outcome kinds as specified in\n   570\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`. Network attachment points are the table entry\n   571\t(gate, value encryption, redaction or metering), the reflection (credential\n   572\tpresentation, value decryption or metering), and the channel (reserved for\n   573\tdeferred channel-level wrapping).\n   574\t\n   575\tKeys and chains are composition data, never descriptor fields. Value-level\n   576\tencryption hides values only; identity, ops, cursors, sizes and timing stay\n   577\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   578\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   579\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   580\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   581\t\n   582\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   583\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   584\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   585\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision.\n   586\t`dao.shibi.md` defines the limit: `verify` adapts a query interpreter to\n   587\tone bounded snapshot, not general tuple-space queries. General query\n   588\texecution and request-specific results are deferred; no wire shape changes.\n   589\t\n   590\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   591\t\n   592\t## 8. Unification\n   593\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   594\tplan lists the successor vocabulary and network-path fates.\n   595\t\n   596\t## 9. Contract boundary\n   597\t\n   598\tThe five `dao.stream.md` amendments are in plan section 2; they add no\n   599\tnetwork or capability concept to the contract.\n   107\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   108\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   109\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   110\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling.\n   111\t\n   112\t`wrap` calls `cursor` with `:dao.stream/oldest` once when it constructs\n   113\tthe gate. The medium must be open and readable; it has capacity one and\n   114\tevicts its older value. If cursor minting is not `ok`, the gate has no read\n   115\tcursor or decision. Each operation retries `cursor` once; if it is still\n   116\tnot `ok`, `next` is skipped and `verify` receives the none marker. A\n   117\tsuccessful mint is its read cursor.\n   118\t\n   119\tOn each operation, the gate calls `next` once at its read cursor. On `ok`,\n   120\tit stores the successor cursor and value. On `blocked`, it keeps the cursor\n   121\tand last value. On `end`, it clears the value and becomes ended. On `gap`,\n   122\tit clears the value, adopts the recovery cursor, and calls `next` once more.\n   123\tIf that call is `ok`, it stores the successor and value; if `blocked`, it\n   124\tkeeps the recovery cursor without a value; if `end`, it becomes ended; if\n   125\t`gap`, it adopts that recovery cursor and remains without a value. It does\n   126\tnot read a third time in this operation. On any other non-`ok` outcome,\n   127\tit clears the value and cursor, then retries cursor minting next operation.\n   128\t\n   129\t`verify` receives the last value observed, or\n   130\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}` when there is none. That value is a\n   131\tbounded snapshot, not a promise that it is the newest concurrent write.\n   132\t`nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits with\n   133\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   134\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   135\trefuses the marker. The cursor and value are state of the\n   136\twrapped handle, supplied to `wrap`, not global state. The composition must\n   137\tserialize calls on one wrapped handle; the gate has no queue, lock or wait.\n   138\tThe decision medium is not the wrapped stream, so the position rule on the\n   139\tinner handle is untouched.\n   140\t\n   141\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   142\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   143\tit was composed with: this is **decision emission**, and an index that\n   144\tobserves that stream is how metered use keeps its state on a stream\n   145\trather than in the gate.\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify` adapter, a pure function of the latest decision, context and\n    26\t  request that answers nil or a reason. This is the query interpreter's\n    27\t  middleware contract, not the query interpreter itself;\n    28\t- an index interpreter over its fact streams, folding with its own cursors\n    29\t  and publishing decisions onto a decision medium a gate reads\n    30\t  (`dao.stream.middleware.md`, gate);\n    31\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    32\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    33\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    34\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    35\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    36\t\n    37\tThe seam directly supports a query interpreter only when it can implement\n    38\t`verify` from one bounded decision snapshot and the request. A general query\n    39\tinterpreter that must query tuple streams needs an additional composition\n    40\tthat runs it over those streams and makes its request-specific result\n    41\tavailable to `verify`. That query execution and its result medium are not\n    42\tspecified here. ShiBi must define them before relying on general tuple-space\n    43\tqueries for authorization; the index and query interpreters remain separate.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    46\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    47\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    48\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    49\t\n    50\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    51\t\n    52\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    53\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    54\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    55\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote Implementation Plan\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: plan, not rules. The rules are\n     4\t[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md); this file carries the\n     5\tmigration design, the completion criteria and slices, and the companion-edit\n     6\tinventory moved out of that spec to keep it short. Subordinate to the spec\n     7\ton any disagreement.\n     8\t\n     9\t## 1. What replaces `dao.jing.remote`: `dao.jing.content`\n    10\t\n    11\tContent lookup is a request and response service over remote streams\n    12\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 5), in one module family, `dao.jing.content`:\n    13\tthe namespaces `dao.jing.content` (`serve-step`), `dao.jing.content.step`\n    14\t(`step`), `dao.jing.content.driver` (`driver`, clj only) and\n    15\t`dao.jing.content.async` (`async`).\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n    18\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n    19\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n    20\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n    21\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text in\n    22\t  the application value; message boundaries are the channel codec's, one\n    23\t  application value per message, so the vocabulary rides Transit-JSON text\n    24\t  or canonical CBOR alike (`dao.jing.cbor.md`, Remote and DHT).\n    25\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n    26\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n    27\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n    28\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n    29\t  moved from today's private `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by\n    30\t  the interpreter, by the stepped client on every `get` answer and\n    31\t  `:present` verify, and by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one\n    32\t  (today: `dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `dao/jing/remote/step.cljc`, and the\n    33\t  linker's own; only the first includes the canonical CBOR decode).\n    34\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n    35\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n    36\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n    37\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n    38\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n    39\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n    40\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n    41\t  `dao.jing` handle. It replaces `connect-content!`; its consumers are the\n    42\t  coordinate opener and, through it, `dao.space` (the published-index\n    43\t  coordinate opens it at `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`) and `yin.repl.link`\n    44\t  on the JVM. `yin.vm.content` has no remote path today (its handle is an\n    45\t  argument), so it gains one only by being handed a coordinate-built handle.\n    46\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n    47\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API;\n    48\t  `dao.data.btree.storage` is the consumer (`hydrate-async`,\n    49\t  `store-tree-async`).\n    50\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n    51\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n    52\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n    53\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone; its consumers are\n    54\t  the tests and the coordinate itself.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 2. Unification and contract boundary\n    57\t\n    58\tFates of the current network paths when remote streams land:\n    59\t\n    60\t- `dao.stream.ws` and its jvm, node, browser and dart adapters: subsumed;\n    61\t  the attachment, writer, deposit medium and projection form a channel.\n    62\t- `dao.stream.serving`: retired; it forwards copies and flattens source gaps.\n    63\t  Its consumers, `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote`, move in slices 4-5.\n    64\t- `dao.stream.forward`: unrelated; it remains a local copy step.\n    65\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: convention over streams; its correlation vocabulary may\n    66\t  remain above reflections.\n    67\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: retired; the WebSocket projection binds the channel.\n    68\t- `dao.stream.apply` wire envelope: retired; the AST node and VM FFI bridge\n    69\t  are unrelated and keep their names.\n    70\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter` and `daostream:ws://`:\n    71\t  convention over request and answer streams; the URL becomes a pair\n    72\t  descriptor. Shared-shell access remains interpreter policy.\n    73\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async` and its coordinate:\n    74\t  deprecated whole; `dao.jing.content` succeeds them in slice 4.\n    75\t- `yin.repl.link` and linker M3/M4: convention over reflections or local\n    76\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core remains.\n    77\t- B-tree `hydrate-async` and `store-tree-async`: convention over the content\n    78\t  client; its callback facade remains host policy under OD-5.\n    79\t- The UDP DHT remains unrelated with its Kademlia envelope; its datagram\n    80\t  budget informs the new UDP channel.\n    81\t- Transit and CBOR remain codecs used by channels.\n    82\t\n    83\tThe `dao.stream.md` changes are the only changes to that contract: OD-1,\n    84\tOD-2 and OD-3; the `:dao.stream/refused` rows on `cursor`, `next` and\n    85\t`append!`; and the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces. They add no network\n    86\tor capability concept. Companion-document edits are listed in section 4.\n    87\t\n    88\t## 3. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n    89\t\n    90\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n    91\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n    92\t\n    93\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    94\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n    95\t+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n   150\t`in` ends that link's channel, outstanding appends are reported\n   151\t`append-unknown`, and `attach!` on the same pair descriptor resumes.\n   152\t\n   153\tDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n   154\t(ShiBi), key distribution, channel-level encryption, a WebRTC data channel,\n   155\tbrowser inbound. None changes a shape in the spec's section 2.\n   156\t\n   157\t## 4. Companion-edit inventory\n   158\t\n   159\tThe edits the unification requires in other design documents, beyond the\n   160\tspec's own sections. Those not yet made are slices; those made in the\n   161\tauthoring and fix rounds are listed as done.\n   162\t\n   163\t- `dao.stream.md` (done): OD-1, OD-2, OD-3 accepted and moved into the\n   164\t  contract body; the `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and\n   165\t  `append!`; the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces; the OD-3 wording\n   166\t  corrected so `attach!`'s `not-found` and a stale cursor are separate\n   167\t  cases.\n   168\t- `dao.lease.md` (done): served entries and relay pairs named as lease\n   169\t  subjects; the carriage note names the `not-found` protocol error.\n   170\t- `dao.stream.ws.md` (done): the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   171\t  the copy path versus attachment to the original; dialer-side and\n   172\t  acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption and RPC items point to the\n   173\t  spec.\n   174\t- `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (done): 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite\n   175\t  the spec; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's source outcome;\n   176\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the acceptance\n   177\t  row cites slice 8 here.\n   178\t- `dao.jing.md` (done): the coordinate shape and its today/target split;\n   179\t  the deprecated backend bullet; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` marked as the\n   180\t  target; the Base64 carriage named as channel-codec message boundaries.\n   181\t- `yin.vm.linker.md` (done): the content pair vocabulary cites section 1\n   182\t  here; the 6.1 table row, 6.3 shape, M3, deps and failure-policy timing.\n   183\t- `dao.data.btree.md` (done): the 5.4 backend row annotated as the target\n   184\t  name for `dao.jing.remote`; the Phase 4 landing note.\n   185\t- `dao.jing.dht.md` (done): the NAT limitation.\n   186\t- `daostream-udp-design.md` (done): superseded status line.\n   187\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (done): the framing claims corrected to channel-codec\n   188\t  message boundaries with Base64 in the application value.\n   189\t- `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, `dao.space.query.md`,\n   190\t  `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md`\n   191\t  (done): one status sentence each naming the successor.\n   192\t- `dao.jing.hash-registry.md`, `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`\n   193\t  (done): one-line pointers that the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe\n   194\t  today's code and the successor keeps the checks and classifications.\n   195\t- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` (done): the minimum viable stack's content\n   196\t  step marked historical, pointing at `dao.jing.content`.\n   197\t- Remaining, with the code: the `dao.stream.ws.md` residual dialer/acceptor\n   198\t  vocabulary beside its older deposit-model wording, and `dao.stream.md`'s\n   199\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   200\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n   201\t\n   202\t## 4. Unification: the fate of every existing path\n   203\t\n   204\tMoved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n   205\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   237\t## The minimum viable stack\n   238\t\n   239\tFor a working multi-agent system on today's code, trusted agents only — no coordinator, no\n   240\tdeposit API, no new namespaces:\n   241\t\n   242\t1. **Historical implementation:** today's `dao.jing.file` content handle\n   243\t   used `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers` and\n   244\t   `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`. `dao.jing.remote` is deprecated\n   245\t   whole; the planned successor is `dao.jing.content` over\n   246\t   `dao.stream.remote.md` (see its implementation plan). Both handles\n   247\t   expose the same plain-data content effects.\n   248\t2. **Writes**: each agent owns a local `dao.stream` memory-log and creates one\n   249\t   transactor value with that stream plus an explicit DaoJing intake pool\n   250\t   (`dao.space.transactor/create!`). It commits entity maps or datom vectors\n   251\t   through `transactor/append!`/`transact!`, using integer stream-local entity ids; the\n     599 docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
