{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '105,140p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '23,50p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '400,412p;567,605p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '54,88p;148,245p'; nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '240,250p'; git diff --check; git diff --unified=0 -- docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | awk '/\"'^'\"\\\\+[\"'^+]/ { line=substr($0,2); if (length(line)>80) print \"WIDE \" length(line) \":\" line; if (line ~ /[''^ -~]/) print \"NONASCII:\" line }'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"     600 docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   108\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   109\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   110\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling. A decision is\n   111\twhatever value the index publishes; the gate reads none of it. It may be an\n   112\timmutable query value, a relation value or an opened published index, in\n   113\twhich case `verify` is a query over it with the request as bindings: the\n   114\tshape `dao.space.query/q` has (a query over immutable inputs, opening\n   115\tnothing), and the required adapter contract for a query interpreter that\n   116\tplugs into the gate (`dao.shibi.md`, How it plugs in).\n   117\t\n   118\t`wrap` calls `cursor` with `:dao.stream/oldest` once when it constructs\n   119\tthe gate. The medium must be open and readable; it has capacity one and\n   120\tevicts its older value. If cursor minting is not `ok`, the gate has no read\n   121\tcursor or decision. Each operation retries `cursor` once; if it is still\n   122\tnot `ok`, `next` is skipped and `verify` receives the none marker. A\n   123\tsuccessful mint is its read cursor.\n   124\t\n   125\tOn each operation, the gate calls `next` once at its read cursor. On `ok`,\n   126\tit stores the successor cursor and value. On `blocked`, it keeps the cursor\n   127\tand last value. On `end`, it clears the value and becomes ended. On `gap`,\n   128\tit clears the value, adopts the recovery cursor, and calls `next` once more.\n   129\tIf that call is `ok`, it stores the successor and value; if `blocked`, it\n   130\tkeeps the recovery cursor without a value; if `end`, it becomes ended; if\n   131\t`gap`, it adopts that recovery cursor and remains without a value. It does\n   132\tnot read a third time in this operation. On any other non-`ok` outcome,\n   133\tit clears the value and cursor, then retries cursor minting next operation.\n   134\t\n   135\t`verify` receives the last value observed, or\n   136\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}` when there is none, or\n   137\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/ended true}` once the gate is ended (the medium\n   138\tclosed; no later operation reads). That value is a bounded snapshot, not a\n   139\tpromise that it is the newest concurrent write.\n   140\t`nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits with\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. The fit to two interpreters is\n    24\tthis, and it is the one path this version chooses:\n    25\t\n    26\t- **Its index interpreter** observes ShiBi's fact streams (grants,\n    27\t  caveats, revocations, emitted decisions) with its own cursors,\n    28\t  materializes them as `dao.space.index` materializes covered indexes, and\n    29\t  publishes the resulting immutable query value, a relation value or an\n    30\t  opened published index, as the decision onto the gate's capacity-1\n    31\t  decision medium (`dao.stream.middleware.md`, gate). The value published\n    32\t  is the tuple space's state at that moment.\n    33\t- **Its query interpreter is `verify`**, and `verify` is the required\n    34\t  adapter contract: a pure function of one immutable query value, the\n    35\t  context and the request, evaluating ShiBi's authorization rules as a\n    36\t  Datalog query with the request as bindings, the shape\n    37\t  `dao.space.query/q` already has (a query over immutable inputs, opening\n    38\t  nothing), and answering nil or a reason.\n    39\t- **A `present`**, a pure function of context and request that produces\n    40\t  the value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`.\n    41\t- **A reason vocabulary** under its own namespace for\n    42\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`.\n    43\t- **Where use is metered**, the decision facts the gate emits, which the\n    44\t  index observes like any other fact stream.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Limits of the adapter contract**, stated precisely. `verify` runs\n    47\tsynchronously inside one operation on one snapshot: its cost per operation\n    48\tis bounded by the composition, and a query over an opened published index\n    49\tresolves only what is already local, because `verify` opens, fetches and\n    50\twaits for nothing; a rule that would need a remote read is a rule the index\n   400\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   401\tunder the inboxes' retention. **An `in` gap ends the channel.** The pair\n   402\tadapter holds the reading cursor on `in`; when `next` there answers `gap`,\n   403\tframes were lost and the adapter cannot say which, so it closes the channel\n   404\treader for this link. The link observes `end` and runs its channel-loss\n   405\tpath (2.4): outstanding ids are abandoned, not re-sent, each abandoned\n   406\t`append!` is reported `append-unknown`, and reattachment is the caller's,\n   407\tby `attach!` on the same pair descriptor, which mints a fresh reading\n   408\tcursor on `in` at `:newest`. The gap is never reported to an inner reader\n   409\tas a source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside\n   410\tan outer `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   411\t\n   412\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   567\tMiddleware preserves positions and outcome kinds as specified in\n   568\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`. Network attachment points are the table entry\n   569\t(gate, value encryption, redaction or metering), the reflection (credential\n   570\tpresentation, value decryption or metering), and the channel (reserved for\n   571\tdeferred channel-level wrapping).\n   572\t\n   573\tKeys and chains are composition data, never descriptor fields. Value-level\n   574\tencryption hides values only; identity, ops, cursors, sizes and timing stay\n   575\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   576\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   577\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   578\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   579\t\n   580\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   581\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   582\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   583\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision. The\n   584\tone ShiBi fit this version chooses: its index interpreter publishes the\n   585\ttuple space's current query value as the decision, and its query\n   586\tinterpreter is `verify`, a query over that value with the request as\n   587\tbindings, the shape `dao.space.query/q` has. `dao.shibi.md` states the\n   588\tadapter contract and its limits (one snapshot, bounded cost, no waiting);\n   589\trequest-specific decisions awaited over streams are a non-goal there.\n   590\t\n   591\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   592\t\n   593\t## 8. Unification\n   594\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   595\tplan lists the successor vocabulary and network-path fates.\n   596\t\n   597\t## 9. Contract boundary\n   598\t\n   599\tThe five `dao.stream.md` amendments are in plan section 2; they add no\n   600\tnetwork or capability concept to the contract.\n    54\t  the tests and the coordinate itself.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 2. Contract boundary\n    57\t\n    58\tThe `dao.stream.md` changes are the only changes to that contract: OD-1,\n    59\tOD-2 and OD-3; the `:dao.stream/refused` rows on `cursor`, `next` and\n    60\t`append!`; and the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces. They add no network\n    61\tor capability concept. Section 5 holds the fate of every network path;\n    62\tsection 4 lists companion-document edits.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 3. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n    65\t\n    66\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n    67\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n    68\t\n    69\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    70\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n    71\t+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n    72\t| 0  | Contract and source alignment: the `dao.stream.md`         | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and the   | clj cljs cljd |\n    73\t|    | amendments of the spec's section 9 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\n    74\t|    | `:dao.stream/refused` added to the closed outcome sets     | throw; the outcome sets admit the refusal.                   |               |\n    75\t|    | for `cursor`, `next` and `append!` in                      |                                                              |               |\n    76\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`; `dao.stream.observe`,          |                                                              |               |\n    77\t|    | `yin.vm.engine` follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.      |                                                              |               |\n    78\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    79\t| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; a     | clj cljs cljd |\n    80\t|    | `present`, a metering exemplar, an index-interpreter        | gate with the channel allow-list refuses with                |               |\n    81\t|    | exemplar publishing decisions. `src/cljc/dao/stream/        | `:dao.stream/refused`; a gate reads the latest decision      |               |\n    82\t|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | through a capacity-1 medium after eviction; a filter cannot  |               |\n    83\t|    |                                                            | be expressed.                                                |               |\n    84\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    85\t| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the channel;     | clj cljs cljd |\n    86\t|    | remote descriptor dispatch, the protocol errors.           | `gap` from an evicting source crosses verbatim with the      |               |\n    87\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, tests.                  | source's cursor; `blocked` then `ok` on re-ask; `not-found`  |               |\n    88\t|    |                                                            | marks gone; `no-surface` does not; the descriptor answer     |               |\n   148\t  acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption and RPC items point to the\n   149\t  spec.\n   150\t- `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (done): 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite\n   151\t  the spec; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's source outcome;\n   152\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the acceptance\n   153\t  row cites slice 8 here.\n   154\t- `dao.jing.md` (done): the coordinate shape and its today/target split;\n   155\t  the deprecated backend bullet; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` marked as the\n   156\t  target; the Base64 carriage named as channel-codec message boundaries.\n   157\t- `yin.vm.linker.md` (done): the content pair vocabulary cites section 1\n   158\t  here; the 6.1 table row, 6.3 shape, M3, deps and failure-policy timing.\n   159\t- `dao.data.btree.md` (done): the 5.4 backend row annotated as the target\n   160\t  name for `dao.jing.remote`; the Phase 4 landing note.\n   161\t- `dao.jing.dht.md` (done): the NAT limitation.\n   162\t- `daostream-udp-design.md` (done): superseded status line.\n   163\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (done): the framing claims corrected to channel-codec\n   164\t  message boundaries with Base64 in the application value.\n   165\t- `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, `dao.space.query.md`,\n   166\t  `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md`\n   167\t  (done): one status sentence each naming the successor.\n   168\t- `dao.jing.hash-registry.md`, `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`\n   169\t  (done): one-line pointers that the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe\n   170\t  today's code and the successor keeps the checks and classifications.\n   171\t- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md` (done): the minimum viable stack's content\n   172\t  step marked historical, pointing at `dao.jing.content`.\n   173\t- Remaining, with the code: the `dao.stream.ws.md` residual dialer/acceptor\n   174\t  vocabulary beside its older deposit-model wording, and `dao.stream.md`'s\n   175\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   176\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n   177\t\n   178\t## 5. Unification: the fate of every existing path\n   179\t\n   180\tMoved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n   181\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   182\t\n   183\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   184\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the spec's 3.1\n   185\t  projection. Its accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become\n   186\t  unnecessary once `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is\n   187\t  slice 3.\n   188\t- `dao.stream.serving`: **retired**. Forwards a source into every accepted\n   189\t  socket and flattens a source `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its\n   190\t  consumers are `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote` (which requires and\n   191\t  drives it); both are retired by slices 4 and 5.\n   192\t- `dao.stream.forward`: **unrelated**. A local copy step; replication by\n   193\t  convention, not exposure.\n   194\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: **convention-over**. Correlation by id over two streams\n   195\t  is the spec's section 5 service; its state machine may be kept as one\n   196\t  such vocabulary over reflections, or retired in slice 5.\n   197\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: **retired**. Its only job is translating `:ws/`\n   198\t  envelopes for rpc; the 3.1 projection is the channel binding now.\n   199\t- `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope: **retired**. Its ok-or-error\n   200\t  wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node\n   201\t  and the VM's FFI bridge keep the name and are unrelated to the network.\n   202\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`, the `daostream:ws://`\n   203\t  URL: **convention-over**. An eval service: `requests` and `answers`\n   204\t  streams, `:op/eval` as payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for\n   205\t  that pair. The shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.\n   206\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`, the `:dao.jing/remote`\n   207\t  coordinate in `dao.jing.coordinate`: **retired**. Deprecated whole;\n   208\t  replaced by `dao.jing.content` (section 1).\n   209\t- `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4: **convention-over**. The\n   210\t  content pair is a request and response service over reflections or local\n   211\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.\n   212\t- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`:\n   213\t  **convention-over**. Consume the content client of section 1; the\n   214\t  callback facade is host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   215\t- `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT: **unrelated**. Raw JVM datagram RPC with its\n   216\t  own Kademlia envelope; its socket rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP\n   217\t  channel's template.\n   218\t- `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`: **subsumed**. The channel\n   219\t  codecs, unchanged.\n   240\tdeposit API, no new namespaces:\n   241\t\n   242\t1. **Historical implementation:** today's `dao.jing.file` content handle\n   243\t   used `dao.jing.remote/default-handlers` and\n   244\t   `dao.jing.remote/connect-content!`. `dao.jing.remote` is deprecated\n   245\t   whole; the planned successor is `dao.jing.content` over\n   246\t   `dao.stream.remote.md` (see its implementation plan). Both handles\n   247\t   expose the same plain-data content effects.\n   248\t2. **Writes**: each agent owns a local `dao.stream` memory-log and creates one\n   249\t   transactor value with that stream plus an explicit DaoJing intake pool\n   250\t   (`dao.space.transactor/create!`). It commits entity maps or datom vectors\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
